.TH SSOOSSHD 8 "2026-08-16" "ssoossh" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
ssoosshd \- SSH certificate authority that issues against an OIDC identity provider
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssoosshd
.RI [ --config
.IR file ]
.br
.B ssoosshd version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ssoosshd
is the trust anchor and policy decision point for SSH access. It
authenticates users through OIDC, optionally enriches identity from LDAP,
and signs SSH public keys into certificates carrying the principals,
critical options, extensions, and validity window that its configuration
allows. Certificate lifetime is derived from issuance context (which
group the requester belongs to, which of the user/host/service/PAM paths
issued it) rather than one global setting, and the server's own
configuration is the outer bound on every option a client or user can
request \(em a client cannot ask for more than the server permits.
.PP
A web UI, served by the same process, handles approval, shows the requester
what was issued and what was trimmed from what they asked for, and gives
users a history of their own certificates.
.PP
.B ssoosshd
issues three kinds of certificate: user (interactive SSH), host (server
identity), and service (non-interactive, structurally a user certificate).
It never receives a private key: keypairs are generated on the client (or,
for enrolled service accounts, supplied by the operator) and only the
public half is ever sent to the server for signing.
.PP
Running
.B ssoosshd
with no arguments starts the server: it loads configuration, connects to
its database, and listens for HTTP(S) or Unix-socket connections per
.BR http. * " in " ssoosshd.yaml (5).
It runs until it receives SIGINT or SIGTERM, at which point it shuts down
gracefully.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "-c, --config " file
Path to the ssoosshd config file to use instead of the normal search path.
See
.BR ssoosshd.yaml (5).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "ssoosshd version"
Prints the server's version, commit, build date, and builder, then exits.
Does not load configuration or start the server.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B ssoosshd
is configured entirely through a YAML file; there are no other flags to set
policy. See
.BR ssoosshd.yaml (5)
for the full schema, defaults, and the file's search order.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT, SIGTERM
Begin graceful shutdown: stop accepting new connections, let in-flight
requests finish, then exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a clean shutdown. Non-zero if configuration fails to load or validate,
if the database or HTTP listener cannot be started, or if the process
receives an error during graceful shutdown.
.SH FILES
Searched in this order; the first file found is used in its entirety (files
are not merged across locations) unless
.B --config
names one explicitly:
.TP
.I ./ssoosshd.yaml
.TP
.I ~/.config/ssoosshd.yaml
.TP
.I ~/.config/ssoossh/ssoosshd.yaml
.TP
.I /etc/ssoosshd.yaml
.TP
.I /etc/ssoossh/ssoosshd.yaml
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssoosshd.yaml (5),
.BR ssoossh (1),
.BR pam_ssoossh (8)
